#!/bin/sh
# Routes Scala text tools to the semantic MCP, and stands down when it cannot.
#
# Reading a .scala source as text is what produces proposals that ignore this repository's
# arrow and abstract-type constraints: text search misses renames, re-exports, implicits and
# inferred uses, and over-matches comments and same-named identifiers. Symbol questions go to
# the ScalaSemantic MCP tools instead.
#
# Two paths, in this order:
#   1. DELEGATION. When the ScalaSemantic-generated guard exists
#      (<root>/.claude/hooks/scala-semantic-guard.sh -- gitignored here, regenerated by
#      `scalasemantic-mcp setup`), this hook translates DSH's lowercase tool names into the
#      Claude Code names that guard matches on and hands it the read decision: exit 0 allows,
#      exit 2 denies with the guard's own reason, and any other exit code stands this hook
#      down with a reason -- a guard that crashed is broken infrastructure, not a verdict.
#      The verdict travels as that exit code; the guard's two streams are merged (2>&1) into
#      the one message fed back to the agent. Never forked, copied or edited.
#   2. FALLBACK. With no generated guard -- a fresh clone or worktree, where .claude/ does
#      not exist at all -- the read decision is made here, mirroring that guard's read half.
#
# Both paths fail open *with a reason*: a read is denied only while .dsh/.state/health says
# healthy=1 (the health hook writes that file once per session), and every stand-down says
# which condition stood it down. The guard must never be the reason a session cannot look at
# its own sources. The semantic index covers .scala sources only, so .mill, .md and everything
# else stays readable, and running a Scala file is not reading it.
#
# PER FILE, NOT PER SESSION -- the guarantee, stated exactly, in three clauses:
#
#   1. A text read is denied only for a file the index COVERS. A read that names ONLY unindexed files
#      stands down: exit 0, one line on stdout naming the file, its Mill module (`sh .dsh/module-map.sh
#      <path>`) and the compile that would index it (`./mill --ticker false --color false
#      <module>.compile`), because a rule that blocks what its own tool cannot answer for is a dead
#      end, not enforcement. Measured in this checkout while this was written: the index covers 66 of
#      164 `.scala` sources (40%), and the number moves upward as modules compile (it was 61 when this
#      rule was first written) -- CAMPAIGN.md's carried gaps own the item, and `health` reports the
#      live figure every session.
#   2. ONE covered operand denies the LINE. `cat COVERED UNCOVERED`, `diff COVERED UNCOVERED` and
#      every other multi-operand reader is denied, exactly as before this rule went per file: the
#      unindexed neighbour does not license reading the covered file, which is the one the guard
#      exists to protect. Read the unindexed file on a line of its own (that line stands down), or
#      release the whole line with the marker below (which is still logged).
#   3. The stand-down is NOT the escape hatch: nothing is denied, so nothing is overridden, no
#      `# semantic-fallback:` marker is needed and .dsh/.state/semantic-fallback.log is not written.
#      The hatch keeps its old meaning and its old log for a COVERED file the guard would otherwise
#      deny.
#   4. A bash line that CHANGES DIRECTORY and names a `.scala`/`.sc` path is UNDECIDED and is denied:
#      every relative name in it resolves against a base this hook does not know, so the same text can
#      mean a file the per-file question is not about (measured: the covered
#      `cd core/src/... && cat ApplicativeField.scala` stood down, exit 0, where the base denied). The
#      rule fires only while a Scala path is named, so `cd core && ./mill core.compile` is untouched.
#   5. A bash token that the CLASSIFIER treats as a Scala operand and that contains a glob
#      metacharacter (`*`, `?` or `[`) names a SET of files, not a spelling of one, and is denied:
#      expanding it would decide about whatever this hook's own cwd happens to match (measured:
#      `grep -n x core/*/ApplicativeField.scala` stood down, exit 0, naming a path spliced out of the
#      checkout's own directories). The classifier's boundary is the rule's boundary: a glob that does
#      not END in `.scala`/`.sc` -- `cat engine/shape/src/*`, `head -3 engine/shape/*`, `cat dir/*` --
#      never classifies, so it is an allow-route and always has been (see the accepted limits).
#      Naming the file, or the semantic tools, are the ways out; the escape hatch below releases it and
#      logs the reason, as it releases any read this guard denies in this in-repo path.
#
# Where the answer comes from: the index files are named after the sources they describe
# (`*/META-INF/semanticdb/<repo-relative source path>.semanticdb`), so coverage is a filename
# lookup. The covered set is cached in .dsh/.state/index-coverage (lib.sh: dsh_coverage_scan), and
# THAT CACHE CANNOT OUTLIVE ITS INDEX, in either direction: it is rebuilt when the index tree's own
# contents change -- a compiled module adds pairs, a `clean` or a restore changes or removes them --
# and an entry is used only while the index file it names is still on disk. The first version of
# this cache keyed staleness on "a `.semanticdb` newer than the cache", which let an older restored
# index through (a covered read ALLOWED) and kept denying after `./mill <module>.clean` (F72's
# deadlock, reintroduced by its own cache). The harness's own fixture trees are pruned exactly as
# the health probe prunes them (F46).
#
# A named path is CANONICALISED before the lookup (`core/./src/A.scala`, `core//src/A.scala`,
# `core/src/../src/A.scala`, `$root/./A.scala`, `~/repo/A.scala` all name the file the index keys),
# and the root prefix is stripped in either spelling (a session may carry the logical or the physical
# path to the same root). A path that is not a path of this repository -- absolute and outside the
# root, or relative and still walking out of it -- stands down: a repo-relative index cannot cover it.
#
# The per-file question is asked only where the call NAMES a file: the file `read`/`grep`/`glob`
# tool points at, or a `.scala`/`.sc` operand in a bash command line. A call that names none -- a
# repo-wide `--type scala` search, a `**/*.scala` glob, a directory `path` -- has no per-file answer
# to give, so the session-wide rule stands for it; that limit is listed below and pinned by fixtures.
#
# ACCEPTED LIMITS (I3/M4) -- what this classifier deliberately does NOT catch, all measured at
# health=1 with the repository's own `cat A.scala` (denied, exit 2) as the control. The rule is
# text-matching over the command line, so it decides about SPELLINGS, never about meaning, and the
# four limits below are the boundary this layer names instead of chasing. C-9 in CONTRACT.md is
# worded to say exactly this much and no more.
#   * An interpreter one-liner that opens the file itself passes, and always will:
#     `python3 -c "print(open('A.scala').read())"`, `perl -e 'print <>' A.scala` and
#     `node -e "fs.readFileSync('A.scala')"` all exit 0 (measured), while `cat A.scala` is denied.
#     Catching them means knowing each language's file APIs -- executing or parsing the command --
#     which is the semantics this layer does not have. Pinned as a PASS by
#     .dsh/tests/cases/semantic-guard-bash-interpreter-read-allowed, so the limit is a decision
#     with a fixture behind it rather than an assumption.
#   * A copy whose destination is not a literal `.scala` path on the same line is missed:
#     `f=A.scala; cp /tmp/x "$f"` writes a source and exits 0 (measured), because no text rule here
#     resolves the variable. The same holds for any indirection (a function, a loop, `$(...)`).
#   * `-t`/`--target-directory` is the documented exception below (a .scala operand is then the
#     SOURCE, not the destination). Its GLUED spelling is not recognised, so `cp -t/tmp/dest A.scala`
#     is denied as a write (measured) where the spaced form is allowed -- a false block, in the safe
#     direction.
#   * The destination rule reads the LAST `.scala`-looking token on the line, so a comment that names
#     one over-denies: `cp A.scala /tmp/backup # see A.scala` exits 2 (measured) although it writes
#     nothing Scala. Same cause, same safe direction: this matches text, not command segments.
#   * Coverage is decided per FILE, so a call that names no file keeps the session-wide rule: with a
#     healthy index, `grep -r --type scala`, a `**/*.scala` glob or a directory-scoped `path` is
#     denied even where the index covers little of what it would search. The classifier has no file
#     to ask about there, and inventing an answer from a directory would be the semantics this layer
#     does not have. Pinned by the grep-glob/path/type fixtures.
#   * A path with a space is recognised only where the command line QUOTES it: `cat "dir with
#     space/S p.scala"` names that one file and is decided on it, while the unquoted spelling is
#     split the way the SHELL itself would split it -- `dir`, `with`, `space/S`, `p.scala` -- and the
#     last of those is what this layer decides about. That is text-matching being honest about being
#     text-matching; pinned as a PASS by
#     .dsh/tests/cases/semantic-guard-bash-unquoted-space-path-limit.
#   * The two undecided verdicts (clauses 4 and 5 above) are text-matching too, and their false
#     positives lean the same way as everything else here -- denied rather than read: a `cd` inside a
#     quoted string or a trailing comment still marks the line as changing directory, and a file whose
#     NAME contains `*`, `?` or `[` is denied as a pattern. The escape hatch releases either, in this
#     in-repo path (with a generated guard installed, the marker is that guard's business and these
#     two verdicts -- reached before delegation -- deny anyway). Pinned by the `semantic-guard-bash-
#     cd-*` and `-bash-glob-*` fixtures, including the two marker cases and one generated-guard case.
#   * A GLOB that does not end in `.scala`/`.sc` is an allow-route, and always has been: the classifier
#     requires a Scala operand to name one, so `cat engine/shape/src/*`, `head -3 engine/shape/*`,
#     `cat engine/shape/src/A.*` and `cat dir/*` exit 0 (measured at this tip AND at the base), while
#     `cat *.sc` and `cat engine/shape/src/*.sc` are operands and are denied by clause 5. That boundary
#     belongs to the classifier, which this round did not touch; the fixture
#     `semantic-guard-bash-glob-not-a-scala-operand-allowed` pins it.
#   * `~user/x` is not expanded (only `~` and `~/`), and a backslash escape inside a quoted operand
#     (`"a\ b.scala"`) is not interpreted: both leave the path exactly as written, so it is looked up
#     as written and stands down rather than denies.
#
# The write half (editing a .scala source) is strict where the generated guard is only strict
# under --strict-edits: an edit of a .scala source is denied outright, and always in-repo. A
# write is never delegated -- a guard installed WITHOUT --strict-edits answers one with a
# reminder and exit 0, an allow that would undo the denial -- so the write verdict does not
# depend on whether such a guard is installed. Reads are unaffected: they are still delegated,
# and they keep their escape hatch.
#
# FROZEN PATHS (lib.sh: dsh_frozen_paths / dsh_frozen_reason) are the human's in-flight work, and
# the write routes are denied for one, with the freeze's own reason quoted: the `write`/`edit` tools
# (any file the call names), a bash write decided on its DESTINATION (a redirect however it is
# quoted, tee, `sed -i`, cp/mv/install/rsync, `dd of=`) -- resolved against the repository root, and
# against a directory the line `cd`d into when that directory is a literal repository path -- and the
# sanctioned annotated-buffer write (`mcp__<server>__annotated_source` CARRYING a `write` argument,
# empty or not: an empty write truncates). The operand-shaped commands (rm/mv/ln/truncate/patch/
# `git checkout|restore|rm|mv|apply`/`perl -i`/`find -delete`) are tool-policy's half of the same
# rule. A freeze is not a semantic-index question, so none of this waits for health=1, and none is
# reachable by the `# semantic-fallback:` marker, which releases reads only. READS ARE NOT AFFECTED by
# a freeze: `annotated_source(uri)` without `write`, and the read/grep/glob/bash readers, keep exactly
# the verdict they had before it.
#
# WHAT THIS LAYER CANNOT SEE is stated in the denial itself, in C-9's discipline, rather than
# oversold: a path built at run time, an interpreter that opens the file itself, a patch whose target
# lives only inside the patch text, and a symlinked spelling. It matches SPELLINGS, never meaning.
set -u
. "$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)/lib.sh"
root=$(gate_root)
# The physical spelling of the same root, for the two places that have to recognise a path a session
# may spell either way: `repo_relpath` below, and the coverage scan's own root normalisation. On
# macOS /tmp is /private/tmp, so a payload, a map row and a find walk can each carry a different but
# equivalent spelling of the root.
root_phys=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$root" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P) || root_phys=$root
tool=$(payload_get tool_name)
# The generated guard, when there is one. Both halves need to know: the read half delegates to
# it, and every write stand-down reports whether it is installed -- a write is never delegated
# to it (F34), so "nothing is enforced here" means something different with a guard present.
generated="$root/.claude/hooks/scala-semantic-guard.sh"
# The repository's own path->module map, consulted by the coverage stand-down so the compile advice
# it prints names the module the BUILD says owns the file rather than a list kept here. It is asked
# once per stand-down (one unindexed read), and it caches its own answer in .dsh/.state/modules.tsv;
# a stale map may make it run `./mill --ticker false moduleMap` once, which is the same exposure the
# post-edit and turn-end gates already carry, and it must never be a reason to skip the advice.
map_script="$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)/module-map.sh"

# The read-half verdict. Mirrors the generated guard's wording closely enough that an agent
# sees the same instruction whichever path produced it.
deny_scala_read() {
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
BLOCKED by semantic-guard (in-repo fallback): text tools are not allowed on .scala sources here.
Text search misses renames, re-exports, implicits and inferred uses, and over-matches
comments and same-named identifiers.
To READ a Scala file, this is the tool:
  annotated_source(uri)
     -> the whole source, plus the inferred types, implicit arguments and conversions the
        compiler resolved, inline. `cat` shows none of that.
For anything else, pick the tool that fits the question:
  symbols / references / types  -> find_symbol, find_usages, type_at_position
  hierarchy / members / givens  -> class_hierarchy, members, resolve_implicits
  signatures / overloads        -> method_signature, find_overloads
  file or project shape         -> document_outline, structure, symbol_source
  literals, comments, TODOs     -> search_text
If the semantic tools genuinely cannot answer this, re-run the READ through Bash with a
trailing `# semantic-fallback: <reason>` marker -- allowed, and appended to
.dsh/.state/semantic-fallback.log so the override stays auditable.
MSG
  exit 2
}

# The write-half verdict. Same text for every route into it -- the `edit`/`write` tools at
# TASK-5-INSERT-POINT and a bash redirect at the tail -- because what the agent needs to be
# told does not depend on how the raw edit was attempted. Mirrors the generated guard's
# --strict-edits message, minus the instruction to reinstall without strict mode: this is the
# in-repo fallback, so there is nothing to reinstall.
deny_scala_write() {
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
BLOCKED by semantic-guard (in-repo fallback): edit .scala sources through the MCP write
path, so the edit is made against the compiler's view of the file rather than raw text:
  1. annotated_source(uri, format="compilable", sentinel=true)
     -> the source with inferred types, implicit args and conversions inline as
        /*SEM:...:SEM*/ blocks (no line-number gutter), plus its sha256
  2. edit that buffer, leaving every SEM block exactly where it is -- they are stripped
     for you, and taking them out by hand edits lines your change does not concern
  3. annotated_source(uri, write=<edited text>, baseHash=<that sha256>)
     -> every SEM block is stripped before the file is saved
Both arguments matter: sentinel=true makes the notes machine-strippable, format=compilable
drops the read-only gutter. Any other read is a view, not a buffer -- writing it back would
persist annotations into the source, and is refused.
A `# semantic-fallback:` marker does NOT exempt a write: that hatch releases READS only, and
no text tool may write a .scala source here. Reads keep it -- re-run a read through Bash with
a trailing `# semantic-fallback: <reason>` and this in-repo path allows it and logs it (with a
generated guard installed, reads are delegated and that guard's own behaviour stands).
MSG
  exit 2
}

# The frozen-path verdict (lib.sh: dsh_frozen_paths / dsh_frozen_reason), one text for the write
# routes -- the `write`/`edit` tools, a bash write, and the semantic server's annotated buffer --
# because what the agent must be told does not depend on which one it tried. A freeze is the HUMAN's
# in-flight work: it is not a semantic-index question, so this deny does NOT wait for health=1 the
# way the Scala write rule does, and no `# semantic-fallback:` marker reaches it (that hatch
# releases READS, and this is a write on every route).
#
# THE MESSAGE STATES THE GUARANTEE EXACTLY, in the discipline C-9 uses: the routes this gate can see
# are listed, and the four classes it cannot see are named as outside it -- so no agent reads "no
# agent may write it, through any route" and believes a variable-built path or a python3 one-liner
# is covered. (The first two lines carry the path and the reason, so they are printed rather than
# heredoc'd; the rest is a QUOTED heredoc, which is what lets the backticks below stay literal under
# dash as well as bash.)
deny_frozen_write() { # <path as the call spelled it> <reason>
  _dw_access=$(dsh_frozen_access "${1:-}" 2>/dev/null || printf frozen)
  _dw_word=FROZEN; [ "$_dw_access" = sealed ] && _dw_word=SEALED
  printf 'BLOCKED by semantic-guard: %s is a %s PATH — the human is working on it right now.\n  reason: %s\n' \
    "$(bound_bytes "${1:-}" 240)" "$_dw_word" "$(bound_bytes "${2:-}" 300)" >&2
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
No agent may write it through the routes this gate can see:
  * the write/edit tools, for any file the call names;
  * a bash write, decided on its DESTINATION — a redirect (`>`/`>>`/`2>`, quoted or not), tee,
    sed -i, cp/mv/install/rsync onto it, dd of= — resolved against the repository root, or against
    the directory an earlier `cd` on the same line entered;
  * the sanctioned annotated buffer, annotated_source(uri, write=...);
  * rm/mv/ln/truncate/patch/git checkout|restore|rm|mv|apply and perl -i naming it, and the
    whole-tree commands (git checkout -- ., git restore ., git clean, git stash, git reset --hard)
    while a freeze exists (tool-policy).
OUTSIDE this layer, by construction — it matches spellings, never meaning:
  * a path built at run time (`f=...; cp /tmp/x "$f"`);
  * an interpreter that opens the file itself (python3 -c "open(p,'w')");
  * a patch whose target lives only inside the patch text (git apply /tmp/p.diff, patch < p.diff);
  * a symlinked spelling of the path (nothing here follows a link);
  * the VALUE of an option that takes one (`install -m 755 <src> <dest>`, `rsync … --exclude foo`):
    it is read as an operand, so a frozen path sitting after such a value can be refused where it is
    only read. That is the safe direction — no destination is missed — and the way out is to name
    the destination first or to use an option that takes no value.
READS STAY ALLOWED: annotated_source(uri) without a write argument, and the read/grep/glob tools,
are unaffected by a freeze — the human's files are still readable.
To let an agent change this path, the HUMAN removes the entry from .dsh/frozen-paths (committed)
or from .dsh/.state/frozen-paths (machine-local), and the change is reviewed like any other.
MSG
  exit 2
}

# The write side of the directory-change rule whose read half is `deny_directory_change_read` below:
# a line that changes directory and writes a RELATIVE destination cannot be decided from the
# destination's spelling alone, because after a `cd` that spelling names a different file than it
# does from the repository root. It is denied, with the way out, rather than resolved by a guess.
#
# The verdict is reached only when the base really is UNKNOWN (lib.sh: dsh_bash_cd_base exits 2): a
# literal `cd engine/details` is resolved and the destination is then decided exactly -- frozen
# entries are refused with their reason -- and a `cd` that provably leaves the repository leaves
# nothing under it frozen, so neither of those reaches this message.
deny_undecided_bash_write() { # <destination as written>
  printf 'BLOCKED by semantic-guard: this line CHANGES DIRECTORY and writes a RELATIVE path (%s), so WHERE it\n' \
    "$(bound_bytes "${1:-}" 240)" >&2
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
writes cannot be decided: after a `cd`, a relative name resolves against that directory, and this
guard resolves against the repository root — so the same text can name a file the frozen-path list
is not about.
Two ways out:
  1. name the destination from the repository root and drop the `cd`:
       printf x > engine/details/notes.md        (no `cd`, no subshell)
     the guard then decides it exactly, and a frozen destination is refused with its reason;
  2. a frozen destination is not released by anything here — the HUMAN removes the entry from
     .dsh/frozen-paths (committed) or .dsh/.state/frozen-paths (machine-local) first.
MSG
  exit 2
}

# The frozen-path verdict for a bash command, from the rows `dsh_bash_action_paths` produced (lib.sh:
# one <kind> TAB <token> row per path the command names as an action, `write` or `cd`). Three
# questions, in this order, because the first is the most precise:
#   1. does the destination's own spelling resolve to a frozen entry (against the repository root)?
#   2. if the line changed directory into a literal repository directory, does the destination
#      resolve to a frozen entry from THERE (`cd engine/details && printf x > notes.md`)?
#   3. if that base is unknown (a variable, `cd ..`, `cd -`, a bare `pushd`), a relative destination
#      is undecided and is denied rather than guessed.
# An absolute destination is decided by (1) alone: no `cd` can move it.
#
# The loop runs in this shell -- not in a pipeline -- so `deny_frozen_write` really exits the hook,
# and globbing is off around the split for the reason the coverage loop turns it off: an unquoted
# expansion would pathname-expand a destination like `engine/details/*` against this hook's own cwd.
deny_frozen_bash_writes() { # <rows from dsh_bash_action_paths>
  _fb_rows=${1:-}
  [ -n "$_fb_rows" ] || return 1
  _fb_base=''
  _fb_base_state=1
  if _fb_base=$(dsh_bash_cd_base "$_fb_rows" "$root"); then
    _fb_base_state=0
  else
    _fb_base_state=$?
  fi
  _fb_tab=$(printf '\t')
  _fb_noglob=1
  case $- in *f*) _fb_noglob=0 ;; esac
  [ "$_fb_noglob" = 1 ] && set -f
  _fb_ifs=$IFS
  IFS='
'
  for _fb_row in $_fb_rows; do
    IFS=$_fb_ifs
    case "$_fb_row" in
      write"$_fb_tab"*) ;;
      *) continue ;;
    esac
    _fb_dest=${_fb_row#*"$_fb_tab"}
    [ -n "$_fb_dest" ] || continue
    if _fb_reason=$(dsh_frozen_reason "$_fb_dest"); then
      deny_frozen_write "$_fb_dest" "$_fb_reason"
    fi
    case "$_fb_dest" in
      /*|'~'|'~'/*) continue ;;   # absolute: decided by its own spelling, wherever the line cd'd
    esac
    if [ "$_fb_base_state" = 0 ]; then
      if _fb_reason=$(dsh_frozen_reason "$_fb_base/$_fb_dest"); then
        deny_frozen_write "$_fb_base/$_fb_dest" "$_fb_reason"
      fi
    elif [ "$_fb_base_state" = 2 ]; then
      deny_undecided_bash_write "$_fb_dest"
    fi
  done
  IFS=$_fb_ifs
  [ "$_fb_noglob" = 1 ] && set +f
  return 1
}

# A SEALED path may not be read either: the holdout suite is judged by the gate, never inspected by
# the agent (a suite it can read is a suite it can special-case). Decided BEFORE health -- a seal is
# not a semantic-index question -- and BEFORE the marker release, which releases reads of the
# semantic guard's own rule only, never a seal. Exit 2, reason on stderr.
deny_sealed_read() { # <path as the call spelled it> <reason>
  printf 'BLOCKED by semantic-guard: %s is a SEALED PATH — the gate runs it; you do not read it.\n  reason: %s\n' \
    "$(bound_bytes "${1:-}" 240)" "$(bound_bytes "${2:-}" 300)" >&2
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
A seal denies READS as well as writes, through every route this gate can see: the read/grep/glob
tools, annotated_source, and a bash line naming the path (or reaching it after a `cd`). The
`# semantic-fallback:` marker does not release it. What you get instead is the turn-end report:
pass/fail counts and the names of failing tests. To open a sealed path, the HUMAN changes its entry
in .dsh/frozen-paths (committed) — a machine-local file can add a seal, never lift one.
MSG
  exit 2
}

# The paths a read-family call names, one per line, for the SEAL verdict -- every operand, not only
# `.scala`/`.sc` (a seal names a path, and a sealed log file is not a source). grep/glob contribute
# their `path` scope and the literal prefix of a `glob`/`pattern` before its first wildcard.
#
# The bash tokenizer here splits on `:` and `=` as well as the shell's separators, which the per-file
# tokenizer below does not: a seal is decided on any spelling that REACHES the path, so `git show
# HEAD:holdout/x`, `--file=holdout/x` and `d=holdout; cat $d/x` must each yield a `holdout…` token.
# Splitting on `=` is also what lets an option's value through the `-`-prefixed skip: `--file` is
# dropped, `holdout/x` is a token of its own.
sealed_read_paths() {
  case "$family" in
    read) printf '%s\n' "$(payload_get tool_input.file_path)" ;;
    grep|glob)
      printf '%s\n' "$(payload_get tool_input.file_path)"
      printf '%s\n' "$(payload_get tool_input.path)"
      _sp_pat=''
      [ "$family" = glob ] && _sp_pat=$(payload_get tool_input.pattern)   # grep's `pattern` is a regex, not a path
      for _sp in "$(payload_get tool_input.glob)" "$_sp_pat"; do
        [ -n "$_sp" ] || continue
        # the part before the first `*`, `?` or `[` -- the directory a pattern is anchored in
        _sp=$(printf '%s' "$_sp" | awk '{ p = $0; i = match(p, /[*?\[]/); if (i > 0) p = substr(p, 1, i - 1); sub(/\/[^\/]*$/, "", p); print p }')
        [ -n "$_sp" ] && printf '%s\n' "$_sp"
      done ;;
    bash)
      printf '%s\n' "$f_command" | awk '
        function flush() { if (tok != "" && tok !~ /^-/ && tok !~ /^#/) print tok; tok = "" }
        {
          n = length($0); q = ""
          for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            c = substr($0, i, 1)
            if (q != "") { if (c == q) { q = "" } else { tok = tok c }; continue }
            if (c == "\047" || c == "\042") { q = c; continue }
            if (c == "#" && tok == "") { break }
            if (c == " " || c == "\t" || index("|;&()<>,:=", c) > 0) { flush(); continue }
            tok = tok c
          }
          flush()
        }' | sort -u ;;
  esac
}

# The seal verdict for the call: every named path, as spelled and (for bash) resolved against the
# directory an earlier `cd` on the line entered. Runs in this shell so deny_sealed_read's exit ends
# the hook.
deny_sealed_reads() {
  _sr_paths=$(sealed_read_paths)
  [ -n "$_sr_paths" ] || return 0
  _sr_base=''; _sr_base_state=1
  if [ "$family" = bash ]; then
    _sr_rows=$(dsh_bash_action_paths "$f_command")
    if _sr_base=$(dsh_bash_cd_base "$_sr_rows" "$root"); then _sr_base_state=0; else _sr_base_state=$?; fi
  fi
  _sr_noglob=1; case $- in *f*) _sr_noglob=0 ;; esac
  [ "$_sr_noglob" = 1 ] && set -f
  _sr_ifs=$IFS; IFS='
'
  for _sr_p in $_sr_paths; do
    IFS=$_sr_ifs
    [ -n "$_sr_p" ] || continue
    if _sr_reason=$(dsh_sealed_reason "$_sr_p"); then deny_sealed_read "$_sr_p" "$_sr_reason"; fi
    case "$_sr_p" in /*|'~'|'~'/*) continue ;; esac
    if [ "$_sr_base_state" = 0 ]; then
      if _sr_reason=$(dsh_sealed_reason "$_sr_base/$_sr_p"); then deny_sealed_read "$_sr_base/$_sr_p" "$_sr_reason"; fi
    fi
  done
  IFS=$_sr_ifs
  [ "$_sr_noglob" = 1 ] && set +f
  return 0
}

# --- per-file coverage: the guard never denies what its own tools cannot answer for -------------
# The concrete source paths this call READS, one per line, in the spelling the call used. For the
# read half of the tool family it is the file the tool points at; for bash it is every `.scala`/`.sc`
# operand of the command text.
#
# The bash splitter is QUOTE-AWARE, which is the whole reason it is not a `tr` + `case` loop: a path
# may contain spaces, and `cat "dir with space/S p.scala"` names ONE file, not `p.scala`. Characters
# between matching quotes are taken verbatim; outside them, whitespace and the shell's separators
# end a token. (The unquoted spelling `cat dir with space/S p.scala` is split the way the SHELL
# splits it -- three operands, the last of which is `p.scala` -- and is a documented accepted limit;
# see the header.)
#
# A call that names no file (a `--type scala` search, a `**/*.scala` glob, a directory `path`) prints
# nothing, and the per-file question then has no answer to give: the session-wide rule stands for it,
# which is what its fixtures pin. awk consumes all of its input, so no writer here is left writing
# into a reader that has gone (F59/F62).
named_read_paths() {
  case "$family" in
    read|grep|glob) printf '%s\n' "$(payload_get tool_input.file_path)" ;;
    bash)
      printf '%s\n' "$f_command" | awk '
        function flush() { if (tok != "" && tok ~ /\.(scala|sc)$/) print tok; tok = "" }
        {
          n = length($0); q = ""
          for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            c = substr($0, i, 1)
            if (q != "") {
              if (c == q) { q = "" } else { tok = tok c }
              continue
            }
            if (c == "\047" || c == "\042") { q = c; continue }
            if (c == " " || c == "\t" || index("|;&()<>,", c) > 0) { flush(); continue }
            tok = tok c
          }
          flush()
        }' | sort -u ;;
  esac
}

# The repository-relative spelling of a named path, or the empty string when it is not a path of
# this repository: absolute and outside the root, or relative and still walking out of it (`../x`).
# Both stand down -- the index is repo-relative, so it cannot cover either, and the semantic tools
# cannot answer for either. A relative path is read against the root, which is the session's cwd in
# production.
#
# The path is CANONICALISED first (lib.sh: dsh_canonical_path), and that is load-bearing rather than
# cosmetic: the index is keyed by one spelling per source, so `core/./src/A.scala`, `core//src/A.scala`
# and `core/src/../src/A.scala` would otherwise all look uncovered and be stood down -- a covered
# read allowed, which is the permissive direction this rule must never drift in. The root prefix is
# then stripped in either of the two spellings a session can carry: the one `gate_root` resolved and
# its physical spelling (`/tmp/...` and `/private/tmp/...` are the same directory on macOS, and a
# payload may name either).
#
# The rule itself is lib.sh's `dsh_repo_relpath`, shared with the frozen-path helpers, so the
# spelling that resolves to a source the index covers and the spelling that resolves to a frozen
# entry are decided by ONE implementation. `$root`/`$root_phys` are this hook's own (resolved once,
# above) because the read path asks the question per named path and must not re-resolve them.
repo_relpath() { # <named path>
  dsh_repo_relpath "${1:-}" "$root"
}

# The stand-down, printed on stdout so it reaches the agent as the reason the read went through: one
# line naming the file, its Mill module and the command that would index it. This is NOT the escape
# hatch and must not be read as one -- nothing is being enforced for this file, so there is nothing
# to override, no marker is needed, and .dsh/.state/semantic-fallback.log is not touched (a marker
# written on an unindexed file is not an override either: this verdict is reached before it).
stand_down_uncovered() { # <named path> <repo-relative path, empty when the path is not ours>
  _su_named=$1
  _su_rel=$2
  if [ -z "$_su_rel" ]; then
    printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down for %s -- it is not a source of this repository (%s), so the semantic index cannot cover it and this read falls back to text; nothing is enforced for this file and no `# semantic-fallback:` marker is needed.\n' \
      "$_su_named" "$root"
    exit 0
  fi
  # module-map answers `<module id>` TAB `<capabilities>`, and only the first field is the module
  # whose compile emits the index. It is handed the ABSOLUTE path -- its own usage line asks for one,
  # and it resolves the path against the root's physical spelling to match the map's module
  # directories, so a relative spelling would be read against the session's cwd instead.
  #
  # Its THREE answers are kept apart, because its own contract does: exit 0 with `-` means "no module
  # owns this path" and is a fact to report; exit 3 means the map is missing or could not be rebuilt,
  # and its header is explicit that this "must not be read as unowned" -- flattening the two would
  # tell the agent the file is nobody's while the truth is that this hook could not find out. The
  # reason travels on stderr and is quoted (bounded) instead.
  _su_module_out=$(sh "$map_script" "$root/$_su_rel" 2>&1)
  _su_module_rc=$?
  _su_module=
  if [ "$_su_module_rc" = 0 ]; then
    _su_module=$(printf '%s' "$_su_module_out" | cut -f1)
  fi
  case "$_su_module" in
    ''|'-')
      if [ "$_su_module_rc" = 0 ]; then
        printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down for %s -- it is not in the semantic index, so the semantic tools cannot answer for it and this read falls back to text; no Mill module owns it, so index the module that should (`./mill --ticker false --color false <module>.compile`; `./mill resolve _` lists them — not `__.compile`, which tool-policy refuses while a holdout suite is sealed). Nothing is denied here and no `# semantic-fallback:` marker is needed.\n' \
          "$_su_rel"
      else
        printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down for %s -- it is not in the semantic index, so the semantic tools cannot answer for it and this read falls back to text; which Mill module owns it could NOT be determined (%s), so index its module with `./mill --ticker false --color false <module>.compile` once the module map is usable (not `__.compile`, which tool-policy refuses while a holdout suite is sealed). Nothing is denied here and no `# semantic-fallback:` marker is needed.\n' \
          "$_su_rel" "$(bound_bytes "$(first_line "$_su_module_out")" 200)"
      fi ;;
    *)
      printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down for %s -- it is not in the semantic index, so the semantic tools cannot answer for it and this read falls back to text; it belongs to Mill module %s, so index it with `./mill --ticker false --color false %s.compile`. Nothing is denied here and no `# semantic-fallback:` marker is needed.\n' \
        "$_su_rel" "$_su_module" "$_su_module" ;;
  esac
  exit 0
}

# A line that changes directory is UNDECIDED when it also names a `.scala`/`.sc` path: every relative
# name in it is resolved against a base this hook does not know, so the same text can mean a different
# file than the one the per-file rule is asked about. `cd core && cat A.scala` reads `core/A.scala`,
# while the guard's own resolution reads `<root>/A.scala` (measured: the covered
# `cd core/src/... && cat ApplicativeField.scala` stood down, exit 0, where the base denied). Denying
# is the safe direction and it is bounded: the rule only fires when a Scala path is named, so
# `cd core && ./mill core.compile` is untouched (the classifier clears the read there anyway).
deny_directory_change_read() {
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
BLOCKED by semantic-guard: this line CHANGES DIRECTORY and names a .scala/.sc source, so which file
it would read cannot be decided. A relative path resolves against the shell's current directory, and
after a `cd` (or a `pushd`, or a subshell) the same text names a different file -- this guard resolves
against the repository root, so it would be deciding about a file the line does not read.
Two ways out:
  1. name the path from the repository root and drop the `cd`:
       cat core/src/.../A.scala        (no `cd`, no subshell)
     the guard then decides per file, and a file the index does not cover stands down with its
     compile command;
  2. use the semantic tools, which take the file itself rather than a shell path:
       annotated_source(uri) for the source, or find_symbol, find_usages, type_at_position,
       class_hierarchy, members, resolve_implicits, method_signature, find_overloads,
       document_outline, structure, symbol_source, search_text for the question you have.
A `# semantic-fallback: <reason>` marker releases this line too, and the reason is appended to
.dsh/.state/semantic-fallback.log: in this in-repo path every read the guard denies can be released,
including one it could not resolve to a file. (With a generated guard installed, the marker is that
guard's business instead -- this verdict is reached before delegation, so the line is denied here and
that guard never sees it.)
MSG
  exit 2
}

# A token containing a glob metacharacter is a SET of files, not a spelling of one, and expanding it
# here would decide about whatever the hook's own working directory happens to match -- measured:
# `grep -n x core/*/ApplicativeField.scala` stood down (exit 0) naming a path spliced out of the
# checkout's own directories. The classifier still decides about a line that names a pattern (it names
# a `.scala` path), and the verdict for it is conservative: a pattern can name covered sources, so it
# is denied, with the two ways out rather than a guess about the caller's cwd.
deny_glob_read() {
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
BLOCKED by semantic-guard: this line names a .scala/.sc SOURCE PATTERN (`*`, `?` or `[`), not a file,
and a pattern can name any number of sources -- including ones the semantic index covers. Expanding it
would decide about whatever this guard's working directory happens to match, which is not what the
command reads.
Two ways out:
  1. name the file (or one file per command):
       cat core/src/.../A.scala
     the guard then decides per file, and a file the index does not cover stands down with its
     compile command;
  2. use the semantic tools, which take the file itself rather than a shell pattern:
       annotated_source(uri), find_symbol, find_usages, type_at_position, class_hierarchy, members,
       resolve_implicits, method_signature, document_outline, structure, symbol_source, search_text.
A `# semantic-fallback: <reason>` marker releases a line like this one and is logged, exactly as it
releases any other read this guard denies -- in this in-repo path. With a generated guard installed the
marker is that guard's business instead, and because this verdict is reached before delegation the
line is denied here and that guard never sees it.
MSG
  exit 2
}

# The escape hatch, in one place, because two verdicts now reach it: a bash read carrying the marker
# is released and logged. Honoured only with no generated guard installed -- with one, the marker is
# that guard's business -- which is the reach the fallback below has always had. Returns 1 (and takes
# no action) when the call is not a marked bash read.
release_with_marker() { # <command>
  [ "$family" = bash ] || return 1
  [ "$mode" = read ] || return 1
  [ -f "$generated" ] && return 1
  case "${1:-}" in
    *'semantic-fallback:'*) ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  mkdir -p "$(state_dir)" 2>/dev/null
  printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')" "read" "$1" \
    2>/dev/null >>"$(state_dir)/semantic-fallback.log"
  exit 0
}

# <uri>: the local path a semantic MCP `uri` argument names. The tool documents a URI; a session
# spells it as a `file://` URL or as a plain path, and a URL may percent-encode a character the path
# really contains (`a#b.scala`, a space), so both the `file://` prefix and `%XX` escapes are undone
# here. The decode is a tiny awk hex table rather than `printf '%b'`: `\xHH` is a bash/ksh extension
# and this file has to run under dash and under the POSIX-mode bash 3.2 that is /bin/sh on macOS.
# A `%` that is not followed by two hex digits is left as written, so nothing is mangled by a
# literal percent.
mcp_local_path() {
  _ml_p=${1:-}
  case "$_ml_p" in
    file://localhost/*) _ml_p=${_ml_p#file://localhost} ;;
    file://*) _ml_p=${_ml_p#file://} ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$_ml_p" | awk '
    # `d` is a function-local (awk has no `local`: extra parameters are the idiom). It must NOT be
    # `i`: a global here would clobber the caller loop index and the walk would never terminate.
    function hexval(c,   d) {
      d = index("0123456789", c)
      if (d > 0) return d - 1
      return index("abcdef", tolower(c)) + 9
    }
    {
      out = ""; n = length($0)
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        c = substr($0, i, 1)
        if (c == "%" && i + 2 <= n && substr($0, i + 1, 2) ~ /^[0-9A-Fa-f][0-9A-Fa-f]$/) {
          out = out sprintf("%c", hexval(substr($0, i + 1, 1)) * 16 + hexval(substr($0, i + 2, 1)))
          i = i + 2
          continue
        }
        out = out c
      }
      printf "%s", out
    }'
}

# --- the sanctioned MCP write route -------------------------------------------------------------
# The annotated-buffer route is a WRITE route: `annotated_source(uri, write=<edited text>,
# baseHash=...)` puts text back into the file, so a frozen path denies it exactly as a raw edit is
# denied -- being the sanctioned route is not a licence to write the human's in-flight work. This
# verdict is reached here, above the family dispatch, for the same reason the write tools are
# decided above every read-only exit: no read path below may answer for a write.
#
# Only a call that CARRIES a `write` argument is decided here. `annotated_source(uri)` alone is a
# READ, and reads of a frozen file stay allowed -- that is the whole point of the freeze being about
# writes: the human's work is still the repository's own source and still has to be readable.
# PRESENCE, not truthiness: an empty `write` is a truncation of the file, not a read, so the test is
# `payload_has` (lib.sh) rather than a non-empty check on the value. (python3/perl one-liners and the
# `# semantic-fallback:` marker are read-side escapes and do not reach a write on any route.)
#
# The tool name is the MCP spelling `mcp__<server>__annotated_source` (the profile names the server
# `scalasemantic`, the Claude Code wiring `scala-semantic`), so the match is on the family: any
# server, this tool. The wiring matchers in .dsh/hooks.json and .dsh/cc-settings.json are the same
# pattern, which is what routes the call here in the first place.
#
# The SEAL half applies to every semantic tool that carries a `uri` and returns source text:
# `source_around_position`, `source_ranges`, `document_outline` and `structure` read the file the
# uri names as surely as `annotated_source(uri)` does, so the same branch decides them (only
# annotated_source has a `write` field, so only it can reach the frozen-write denial). Symbol-based
# tools (`symbol_source`, `find_usages` with contextLines, `search_text`) name a SYMBOL, not a path,
# and are outside what a name guard can decide -- see the README.
case "$tool" in
  mcp__*__annotated_source|mcp__*__source_around_position|mcp__*__source_ranges|mcp__*__document_outline|mcp__*__structure)
    mcp_uri=$(payload_get tool_input.uri)
    mcp_path=$(mcp_local_path "$mcp_uri")
    if payload_has tool_input.write && [ -n "$mcp_uri" ]; then
      if mcp_reason=$(dsh_frozen_reason "$mcp_path"); then
        deny_frozen_write "$mcp_path" "$mcp_reason"
      fi
    fi
    if [ -n "$mcp_uri" ]; then
      if mcp_reason=$(dsh_sealed_reason "$mcp_path"); then
        deny_sealed_read "$mcp_path" "$mcp_reason"
      fi
    fi
    exit 0 ;;
esac

# --- tool family dispatch ---------------------------------------------------------------
# A family, never a spelling: DSH registers the lowercase names and Claude Code the
# capitalized ones, and one gate.sh serves both, so `read` and `Read` must decide alike.
case "$tool" in
  read|Read) family=read ;;
  grep|Grep) family=grep ;;
  glob|Glob) family=glob ;;
  bash|Bash) family=bash ;;
  write|edit|Write|Edit|MultiEdit)
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- TASK-5-INSERT-POINT
    # The write half, decided HERE -- above every read-only early exit, so the read path
    # below never runs for a write tool, and never handed to a generated guard (F34): one
    # installed without --strict-edits only *reminds* on a write, and its allow would undo
    # this denial. The file being written is `payload_get tool_input.file_path`.
    #
    # `NotebookEdit` is deliberately NOT in the family, exactly as in post-edit.sh (M5): Claude
    # Code's matcher lists for this hook name it, but a notebook payload carries `notebook_path`
    # and not `tool_input.file_path`, so this branch would read an empty path, match neither
    # `.scala` nor `.sc`, and exit 0. That is the correct verdict rather than a hole -- a notebook
    # cannot be a `.scala`/`.sc` source, and this hook decides about nothing else -- and it is
    # asserted in .dsh/tests/vocabulary-test.sh, so the no-op stays a decision.
    file_path=$(payload_get tool_input.file_path)
    # A FROZEN path is denied first -- above the annotated-buffer route below (the buffer is a
    # write path too, so the sanctioned route is not a way round a freeze) and above the health
    # gate (a freeze is not a semantic-index question: it holds whether or not the index is
    # healthy, and the `# semantic-fallback:` marker releases reads only, never a write).
    # The verdict is asked for EVERY file the tool names, not only `.scala`/`.sc`, because a
    # freeze names a path: writing `engine/details/notes.md` is writing the human's module.
    if [ -n "$file_path" ]; then
      if frozen_reason=$(dsh_frozen_reason "$file_path"); then
        deny_frozen_write "$file_path" "$frozen_reason"
      fi
    fi
    case "$file_path" in
      *.scala|*.sc)
        # Fail open *with a reason* while the semantic index is not healthy: denying the edit
        # then would leave the repository unreadable AND unwritable, the deadlock the read
        # half already learned to avoid (F33). The line names the operation being allowed and
        # whether a generated guard is installed, exactly as the fallback gate's write line
        # does (F35): a write is never delegated to that guard, so its presence is worth
        # stating, and its absence is one of the two reasons this call went through.
        if grep -q '^healthy=1$' "$(state_dir)/health" 2>/dev/null; then
          deny_scala_write
        fi
        if [ -f "$generated" ]; then
          printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down -- health is not healthy=1, so the write of %s is allowed and nothing is enforced here; a generated guard is installed at %s, but a write is never delegated to it.\n' "$file_path" "$generated"
        else
          printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down -- health is not healthy=1 and no generated guard is installed, so the write of %s is allowed and nothing is enforced here.\n' "$file_path"
        fi
        exit 0 ;;
    esac
    exit 0 ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

# --- classify: does this call target a Scala source, and is it a read or a write? --------
# Mirrors the generated guard's read half, with two DOCUMENTED DRIFTS: a search pinned to one
# source reads `file_path` too, and the write half below recognises the copying commands. The
# reference mirrors the hints the tool itself emits; this classifier enforces this repository's
# rule, so where the two disagree the rule wins. `.scala`/`.sc` has to END a path, not merely
# appear in one: `mill.scalalib`, `scalafmt` and `.scala-build` are not sources, and
# blocking them would block the build itself.
mode=
f_command=
case "$family" in
  read)
    case "$(payload_get tool_input.file_path)" in
      *.scala|*.sc) mode=read ;;
    esac ;;
  grep|glob)
    # Only when the call itself names Scala: an unscoped repo-wide search may legitimately
    # be after comments, config or non-Scala files. All four fields DSH and Claude Code use
    # to scope a search are read, `file_path` included (a search pinned to one source).
    case "$(payload_get tool_input.file_path)$(payload_get tool_input.glob)$(payload_get tool_input.path)$(payload_get tool_input.type)" in
      *scala*) mode=read ;;
    esac ;;
  bash)
    f_command=$(payload_get tool_input.command)
    if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq '\.(scala|sc)([^[:alnum:]_-]|$)'; then
      # A text reader is what makes this scraping the source rather than running it. The list is the
      # commands that DUMP CONTENT: the search and display tools, plus the filters and encoders that
      # print back what they are handed. It was ten words wide and measured leaky (I3) -- at health=1,
      # with `cat` and `head` denying at 2 as controls, `sort`, `diff`, `cut`, `od`, `strings`, `fold`
      # and every other word added here read a `.scala` source at exit 0. The guarantee stays
      # text-matching, not semantics: see the accepted limits in this file's header.
      if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq \
        '(^|[|;&(`]|[[:space:]])(grep|rg|ag|ack|cat|sed|awk|head|tail|less|more|nl|sort|diff|cut|od|strings|fold|xxd|hexdump|split|csplit|paste|join|comm|uniq|tr)([[:space:]]|$)'
      then
        mode=read
      fi
      # Handing the file to a runner is executing it, not reading it -- and the pipeline
      # that filters its OUTPUT (`scala-cli foo.sc | grep ...`) is not a text search either.
      if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq \
        '(^|[|;&(`/]|[[:space:]])(scala-cli|scala|scalac|amm|mill|sbt|java)([[:space:]]|$)'
      then
        mode=
      fi
      # `git log/show/diff/blame ... -- foo.scala` names the file only as a pathspec or rev
      # filter: git reads its own object store, not the working-tree source text.
      if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq '(^|[|;&(`]|[[:space:]])git([[:space:]]|$)'; then
        mode=
      fi
      # A redirect, or an in-place edit, whose TARGET is the Scala file is a write rather
      # than a read -- and it outranks a reader on the same line (`cat > A.scala`).
      if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq \
        '(>>?|tee)[[:space:]]*"?[^[:space:]"]*\.(scala|sc)([[:space:]"]|$)|sed[[:space:]]+-i[^|;&]*\.(scala|sc)([[:space:]]|$)'
      then
        mode=write
      fi
      # DOCUMENTED DRIFT from the reference guard, which stops at the redirects above: it
      # mirrors the hints the tool itself emits, while this classifier enforces this
      # repository's rule -- and `cp`, `mv`, `install`, `rsync` and `dd of=` put a file where a
      # .scala source lives just as surely as `>` does.
      # The DESTINATION decides, not any .scala named: `cp A.scala /tmp/backup` reads a source
      # into a non-Scala file and stays allowed, while `cp /tmp/x A.scala` overwrites one. A
      # destination is the command's LAST operand, so the pattern wants the .scala path
      # followed by nothing but trailing redirects, an optional trailing comment, a separator
      # or the end of the command -- which is also what keeps `cp -r dir /tmp/snapshot` and
      # `rsync a.scala /tmp/dest/` out. The comment is spelled out because that is what a
      # `# semantic-fallback:` marker IS: anchoring on the separator alone let
      # `cp /tmp/x A.scala # semantic-fallback: backup` through, the one thing a marker must
      # never do. Either quote style may wrap a path, hence the spliced ['"] classes.
      #
      # `-t`/`--target-directory` is the exception: it names the DESTINATION itself, so a
      # .scala operand is the source being copied in and not a write. Only `cp`/`mv`/`install`
      # take it that way (`rsync -t` is --times), and it is honoured for the whole command line
      # because this classifier matches text, not command segments -- a second, separate copy
      # onto a .scala path on the same line is therefore missed, the documented limit of this
      # layer. A flag whose own argument ends in .scala still denies, because that does write a
      # .scala path.
      if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq \
        '(^|[|;&(`]|[[:space:]])(cp|mv|install)([[:space:]]+[^[:space:]|;&]*)*[[:space:]]+(-t|--target-directory)([[:space:]=]|$)'
      then
        if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq \
          '(^|[[:space:]])(-t|--target-directory)[[:space:]=]+['"'"'"]?[^[:space:]"]*\.(scala|sc)['"'"'"]?'
        then
          mode=write
        fi
      elif printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq \
        '(^|[|;&(`]|[[:space:]])(cp|mv|install|rsync)([[:space:]]+[^[:space:]|;&]*)*[[:space:]]+['"'"'"]?[^[:space:]"]*\.(scala|sc)['"'"'"]?([[:space:]]*(>>?|2>>?|&>)[[:space:]]*[^|;&[:space:]]*)*([[:space:]]*#.*)?[[:space:]]*([|;&]|$)' \
        || printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq \
        '(^|[|;&(`]|[[:space:]])dd[[:space:]][^|;&]*of=[[:space:]]*['"'"'"]?[^[:space:]"]*\.(scala|sc)['"'"'"]?([[:space:]]|$)'
      then
        mode=write
      fi
    fi ;;
esac

# --- frozen paths: a bash write into the human's in-flight work ---------------------------------
# The classifier above answers about `.scala`/`.sc`; a freeze names a PATH, so this verdict is its
# own question, asked of every destination the command writes whatever the destination is called
# (`cp /tmp/x engine/details/notes.md` writes the human's module just as surely as a `.scala` copy
# does), AND of the same destination resolved against a directory the line changed into. It is
# reached BEFORE the coverage stand-down -- a frozen file the index does not cover must still be
# denied -- and before the marker release, which releases reads only. It does not wait for health=1:
# a freeze is not a semantic-index question, and the human's in-flight work is protected in a session
# whose index is not built yet exactly as it is in one whose index is.
#
# The rows are computed ONCE (lib.sh: dsh_bash_action_paths) and handed to the verdict, which reads
# the `write` rows and asks `dsh_bash_cd_base` for the `cd` rows: one awk per bash call, not two.
if [ "$family" = bash ] && [ -n "$f_command" ]; then
  frozen_action_rows=$(dsh_bash_action_paths "$f_command")
  deny_frozen_bash_writes "$frozen_action_rows" || :
fi

# --- sealed reads: denied before health and before the marker ---------------------------------------
# `mode` is not consulted: a seal names a path, whatever its extension, and a read of it is denied
# whether or not the classifier would have called it a Scala read.
case "$family" in read|grep|glob|bash) deny_sealed_reads ;; esac

# The operation, in words, for the stand-down lines below (F35). They are the audit record of
# why a call was allowed through, and describing a write as "the read" hides which rule stood
# down; a call this hook does not classify is neither, and says so.
operation=call
case "$mode" in
  read) operation=read ;;
  write) operation=write ;;
esac

# --- per-file coverage: stand down for a file the index does not cover --------------------------
# This runs BEFORE delegation on purpose. With a generated guard installed the read would be handed
# to a guard whose deny rests on the same existence-based premise, so the per-file verdict has to be
# reached here, before either decision-maker can deny on it -- otherwise the deadlock this block
# exists to remove would still be there for exactly the sessions that install that guard.
#
# Health first: an unhealthy session already stands down with its own reason below (and for a write,
# F33/F34), and a session with no index must not pay for a coverage scan whose answer it cannot use.
# Writes are not classified here at all: coverage is a question about reading a source, and the write
# half keeps its own rule.
if [ "$mode" = read ] && grep -q '^healthy=1$' "$(state_dir)/health" 2>/dev/null; then
  coverage_cache="$(state_dir)/index-coverage"
  dsh_coverage_scan "$root" "$coverage_cache" >/dev/null 2>&1
  # One path per line, so IFS is narrowed to newline: a path may contain a space, and splitting on
  # the default IFS would ask the cache about two halves of it and stand down for a covered file.
  # The loop runs in this shell (a `while read` pipeline would run in a subshell, where `exit 0` in
  # stand_down_uncovered would end the subshell and let the deny below run anyway).
  #
  # ANY covered operand DENIES the line. The first version stood down as soon as one operand was
  # uncovered, so `cat COVERED UNCOVERED` -- and every multi-operand reader, `diff`, `sort`, a
  # two-file `head` -- exited 0 where the rule before this change denied: the covered file is
  # exactly what the guard exists to protect, and an unindexed neighbour does not license reading it.
  # The unindexed file is still readable on its own line, and the escape hatch still releases a mixed
  # line in this in-repo path (with its marker, and it is still logged; with a generated guard
  # installed the read is delegated and that guard's own behaviour stands).
  #
  # So the stand-down is for the case where the line names paths and NONE of them is covered; the
  # first such path is the one reported, because it is the one the agent asked about.
  coverage_names=$(named_read_paths)
  coverage_any_covered=0
  coverage_first_named=''
  coverage_first_rel=''
  coverage_glob=0
  # GLOBBING IS OFF around the split for the same reason it is off inside dsh_canonical_path: the
  # expansion is unquoted (IFS is a newline, so a path may contain a space), and an unquoted
  # expansion would also PATHNAME-expand every token against this hook's own cwd -- which is how
  # `core/*/A.scala` became a path spliced out of the checkout. Tokens stay the spellings the
  # command line carries; a token that IS a pattern is then denied below rather than expanded.
  coverage_noglob=1
  case $- in *f*) coverage_noglob=0 ;; esac
  [ "$coverage_noglob" = 1 ] && set -f
  coverage_ifs=$IFS
  IFS='
'
  for coverage_named in $coverage_names; do
    [ -n "$coverage_named" ] || continue
    case "$coverage_named" in
      *'*'*|*'?'*|*'['*) coverage_glob=1 ;;
    esac
    coverage_rel=$(repo_relpath "$coverage_named")
    if [ -n "$coverage_rel" ] && dsh_coverage_covers "$root" "$coverage_cache" "$coverage_rel"; then
      coverage_any_covered=1
      break
    fi
    if [ -z "$coverage_first_named" ]; then
      coverage_first_named=$coverage_named
      coverage_first_rel=$coverage_rel
    fi
  done
  IFS=$coverage_ifs
  [ "$coverage_noglob" = 1 ] && set +f
  # The verdicts that are about the LINE rather than about one file come first, because they are the
  # ones that say the per-file question cannot be answered: a directory change, then a pattern. Both
  # are bash-only -- the `read`/`grep` tool hands over one concrete path, and a pattern in that field
  # is not expanded by anything here (globbing is off), so it is simply not in the cache.
  if [ "$family" = bash ]; then
    if printf '%s' "$f_command" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq '(^|[|;&(`]|[[:space:]])(cd|pushd)([[:space:]]|$)'; then
      release_with_marker "$f_command" || deny_directory_change_read
    fi
    if [ "$coverage_glob" = 1 ]; then
      release_with_marker "$f_command" || deny_glob_read
    fi
  fi
  # Empty means "the call named no file at all" -- the session-wide rule stands for those.
  if [ "$coverage_any_covered" = 0 ] && [ -n "$coverage_first_named" ]; then
    stand_down_uncovered "$coverage_first_named" "$coverage_first_rel"
  fi
fi

# --- delegation: the generated guard, when there is one, owns the read decision ----------
# Reads only. A call the classifier above called a WRITE never gets here (F34): a generated
# guard denies a write only when it was installed with --strict-edits, and one installed
# without it answers with a reminder and exit 0 -- an allow that would undo the in-repo
# denial, which is the one thing strict mode exists to prevent. Calls this hook does not
# classify at all (an unscoped bash command) are still delegated.
if [ -f "$generated" ] && [ "$mode" != write ]; then
  # Fail open when the semantic answer is not actually available: without a healthy index
  # the generated guard could only guess, and standing down beats blocking a read.
  if ! grep -q '^healthy=1$' "$(state_dir)/health" 2>/dev/null; then
    printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down -- health is not healthy=1, so the generated guard at %s is not enforced and the %s is allowed.\n' "$generated" "$operation"
    exit 0
  fi
  # Not `$(case ... esac)`: the POSIX-mode bash 3.2 that is /bin/sh on macOS refuses to
  # parse a case statement inside a command substitution, and this hook must run there.
  case "$family" in
    read) cc_tool=Read ;;
    grep) cc_tool=Grep ;;
    glob) cc_tool=Glob ;;
    bash) cc_tool=Bash ;;
    *) cc_tool= ;;
  esac
  out=$(payload_with_tool "$cc_tool" | CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR="$root" sh "$generated" 2>&1)
  code=$?
  # Dispatch on the exit code exactly as gate.sh does, because that is what the exit code
  # means to the bridge: 0 allows, 2 denies with the reason on the message, and anything
  # else is a non-blocking error -- propagating it would be a refusal with no reason, the
  # fail-closed deadlock this harness exists to remove.
  case "$code" in
    0)
      if [ -n "$out" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$out"; fi
      exit 0 ;;
    2)
      printf '%s\n' "$out" >&2
      exit 2 ;;
    *)
      printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down -- the generated guard %s exited %s, which is neither 0 (allow) nor 2 (deny), so its decision is not enforced and the %s is allowed.\n' "$generated" "$code" "$operation"
      if [ -n "$out" ]; then printf 'DSH semantic-guard: that guard said:\n%s\n' "$out"; fi
      exit 0 ;;
  esac
fi

# --- in-repo fallback -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reached only when .claude/hooks/scala-semantic-guard.sh is absent -- the ordinary case in
# this repository, since .claude/ is gitignored and no worktree or fresh clone has it.
# The `# semantic-fallback:` marker is the auditable escape hatch, and it is honoured here
# only: with a generated guard installed, that guard's own logging behaviour stands. The call
# is classified FIRST, so the marker releases and logs a read -- never a write, which falls
# through to the write branch, and never a call this guard does not classify at all.
release_with_marker "$f_command" || :

# Fail open, per the brief: a deny needs a healthy semantic index behind it. Say so, rather
# than standing down in silence -- an unexplained non-decision is what makes a gate untrusted.
# Reaching here means delegation did not run, for one of exactly two reasons, and the line
# names the one that applies plus the operation being allowed (F35): "the read" was a lie about
# a write, and "no generated guard is installed" is a lie when a guard is installed and the
# call was a write, which is never delegated to it (F34).
if ! grep -q '^healthy=1$' "$(state_dir)/health" 2>/dev/null; then
  if [ "$mode" = write ] && [ -f "$generated" ]; then
    printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down -- health is not healthy=1, so the write is allowed and nothing is enforced here; a generated guard is installed at %s, but a write is never delegated to it.\n' "$generated"
  else
    printf 'DSH semantic-guard: standing down -- health is not healthy=1 and no generated guard is installed, so the %s is allowed and nothing is enforced here.\n' "$operation"
  fi
  exit 0
fi

case "$mode" in
  read) deny_scala_read ;;
  write)
    # A write to a Scala source, classified for bash by the in-repo classifier above:
    # `cat > A.scala`, `cat >> A.scala`, `sed -i ... A.scala`. Delegation skipped it on
    # purpose (F34), and the health gate just above stood this hook down with a reason if the
    # index is unhealthy (F33) -- so a write that reaches this line is denied, with the same
    # message the write tools get at TASK-5-INSERT-POINT.
    deny_scala_write ;;
esac
exit 0
